# Loombre :: installers/docker/loombre.env.example
#
# Copy this file to `loombre.env` (same directory), fill in the values
# marked REQUIRED, then run compose with:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file installers/docker/loombre.env up -d
#
# `loombre.env` itself is gitignored (see .dockerignore/.gitignore) — never
# commit real secrets. Every variable docker-compose.prod.yml reads is
# documented below; anything left unset falls back to the default noted.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres (docker-compose.prod.yml's `postgres` service)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# REQUIRED. No default — compose refuses to start without this set.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Optional. Database/role name inside the postgres container. Change these
# together with DATABASE_URL below if you point at an external Postgres
# instead of the bundled one (see "External Postgres" in docs/install/docker.md).
POSTGRES_DB=loombre
POSTGRES_USER=loombre

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server — required
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# REQUIRED (docker-compose.prod.yml's `:?` guard refuses to start without
# it). Signs access JWTs (docs/PLAN.md §10). Only the SERVER reads this —
# the worker never does, so there is no cross-process agreement concern
# (this file used to claim otherwise; stale prose corrected by the
# installer completeness audit). Also corrected: since P4.7/P4.17 an unset
# secret no longer means "new ephemeral secret every process start" — the
# server generates one and PERSISTS it (in this stack: under /data/secrets
# on the `loombre_data` volume, via @loombre/secrets), so restarts keep
# every outstanding token. The compose file still requires an explicit
# value anyway: a secret HERE survives `docker compose down -v` (which
# wipes /data — a volume-persisted secret dies with it, logging everyone
# out) and rides along with whatever backup captures the rest of this
# file. Generate one with: `openssl rand -base64 48`
LOOMBRE_JWT_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server — networking / remote access (docs/PLAN.md §10, README.md's
# "Remote access" section — same posture applies to the Docker path)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Host port the server's HTTP API is published on (container-internal PORT
# matches this same value — see docker-compose.prod.yml). Default 3001
# matches the server's own built-in default (apps/server/src/main.ts).
# Real deployments should NOT expose this directly to the internet — put a
# reverse proxy in front (80/443 + TLS) and leave this bound to localhost/
# your LAN. See docs/install/docker.md.
LOOMBRE_PORT=3001

# Comma-separated allowed CORS origins for the web client — the origins
# BROWSERS load the web UI from, exactly as they appear in the address
# bar. UNSET and EMPTY differ (the compose file uses the `-` operator
# specifically to preserve that distinction — verified against
# apps/server/src/main.ts resolveCorsOrigins):
#   - Unset (this line commented out, the shipped default): compose
#     supplies the bundled `web` container's localhost pairing
#     (http://localhost:<web port> + http://127.0.0.1:<web port>,
#     tracking LOOMBRE_WEB_PORT) — works for a browser running ON the
#     Docker host, and for nothing else.
#   - Set: your real web origin(s) — REQUIRED for LAN/remote browsers,
#     e.g. LOOMBRE_CORS_ORIGINS=http://192.168.1.20:3000. Pair with
#     LOOMBRE_SERVER_ORIGIN below (the same relationship, inverted).
#   - Empty (uncomment, leave blank): disables CORS entirely — the
#     same-origin posture behind ONE reverse proxy serving web UI and API
#     from a single origin (the recommended real deployment).
# LOOMBRE_CORS_ORIGINS=

# Set ONLY when running behind a reverse proxy YOU control that strips/
# overwrites incoming X-Forwarded-For before adding its own (nginx/Caddy/
# Traefik in front of this compose stack). Values: "1" (trust one hop),
# an Express preset ("loopback"), or a CIDR list ("10.0.0.0/8"). Leaving
# this unset is safe with no configuration — see README.md's "Remote
# access" section for why setting it WITHOUT a real proxy in front is a
# spoofing risk.
LOOMBRE_TRUST_PROXY=

# Host UDP port published for Loombre Remote's embedded WireGuard listener
# (docs/ops/remote-access/loombre-remote.md). Inert until an admin enables
# the Remote path in Settings → Remote Access — nothing listens on it
# before that. Must match the `remote.wireguardPort` setting (default
# 51820); if you change one, change both. This is also the UDP port the
# setup wizard's router instruction card tells you to forward.
# LOOMBRE_WG_PORT=51820

# The public URL of THIS Loombre instance as your users reach it (e.g.
# https://media.example.com) — env pin for the `network.publicUrl`
# setting. Links in outgoing mail (invites, password resets) are built
# from it, and mail sending stays OFF until it is set (here or in the
# admin UI — Settings → Mail, which is the normal path; see the Mail
# block below). Leave unset to manage it from the UI. NOTE: enabling the
# Tunnel remote-access path auto-fills the SETTING when it is empty; an
# env pin here overrides that and locks the UI field.
# LOOMBRE_PUBLIC_URL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mail (SMTP) — OPTIONAL env pins (settings registry `mail.*`, optional
# mail transport run E1/E5). Everything mail works with ALL of these
# unset: invites and password recovery function without mail entirely, and
# when you do want mail, Settings → Mail in the admin UI is the normal
# way to configure it (host/port/security/from live in the database;
# username/password go to the OS keyring via the write-only credentials
# card). Set these ONLY to pin mail config by environment — a set value
# locks the matching UI field (empty = unset, locks nothing).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SMTP relay host + port + security. Security is one of: starttls
# (default, port 587), implicit-tls (port 465), none (port-25 LAN relays
# only — the UI shows a caution for a reason).
# LOOMBRE_SMTP_HOST=
# LOOMBRE_SMTP_PORT=587
# LOOMBRE_SMTP_SECURITY=starttls

# Sender identity on outgoing mail.
# LOOMBRE_SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=
# LOOMBRE_SMTP_FROM_NAME=Loombre

# SMTP AUTH credential pair — set BOTH or NEITHER (a lone half is
# rejected; unauthenticated relays legitimately need neither). These two
# are keyring-class secrets, not registry settings: setting them disables
# the UI credentials card (409 env-pinned) for as long as they are set.
# Same handling caveat as LOOMBRE_JWT_SECRET above — this file holds a
# real credential once filled in.
# LOOMBRE_SMTP_USERNAME=
# LOOMBRE_SMTP_PASSWORD=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Web client (docker-compose.prod.yml's `web` service — the browser UI,
# added by the installer completeness audit; a Next.js standalone server
# in its own image, separate from server/worker)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Host port the web UI is published on (container-internal PORT matches —
# same convention as LOOMBRE_PORT above). The first-run setup wizard and
# everything else you'd call "the app" live here, NOT on the API port.
LOOMBRE_WEB_PORT=3000

# The API/server origin(s) the web client's Content-Security-Policy lets
# the BROWSER call (connect/img/media-src — apps/web/src/lib/csp.ts, the
# LOOMBRE_CORS_ORIGINS pairing's other half). Must be the origin browsers
# actually connect to, never a container-internal name. UNSET and EMPTY
# differ, same as LOOMBRE_CORS_ORIGINS above:
#   - Unset (commented out, the shipped default): compose defaults it to
#     http://localhost:<LOOMBRE_PORT> — right only when the browser runs
#     on the Docker host itself.
#   - Set: the externally visible server origin for LAN/remote use, e.g.
#     LOOMBRE_SERVER_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.20:3001.
#   - Empty (uncomment, leave blank): the documented scheme-wildcard CSP
#     fallback (http:/https:/ws:/wss:) — the multi-server posture; safe
#     when you cannot enumerate origins up front.
# Mismatch symptom: login page loads, but every API call dies with CSP
# violations in the browser console.
# LOOMBRE_SERVER_ORIGIN=

# Where the SERVER's own "open the web UI" surfaces point
# (IpcStatusResponse.webUrl / GET /ipc/v1/open-web-target —
# apps/server/src/ipc/web-url.ts). Unset = compose defaults it to
# http://localhost:<LOOMBRE_WEB_PORT>. Set it to the same externally
# visible web origin you put in LOOMBRE_CORS_ORIGINS when browsers reach
# this instance over the LAN / a reverse proxy.
# LOOMBRE_WEB_URL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server — performance tier + playback policy (docs/PLAN.md §9.2)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "0" | "1" | "2" (docs/PLAN.md §9.1 hardware tiers). Unset = server infers
# defaults from resolved capabilities rather than a fixed tier. Set this
# explicitly if you know your host is Tier-0 class (N100/Pi-5/4 GB RAM) so
# concurrency caps and the transcode ladder default conservatively without
# needing a first-boot probe to discover that.
LOOMBRE_TIER=

# "true" | "false". Unset = transcoding allowed (subject to
# LOOMBRE_MAX_TRANSCODES and the resolved hardware capabilities). Set to
# "false" to force direct-play-only (e.g. a box with no usable hw encoder
# and CPU headroom you don't want transcoding to consume).
LOOMBRE_ALLOW_TRANSCODE=

# Positive integer. Overrides the tier-derived default for how many
# simultaneous transcode sessions the server will admit (429 beyond this —
# docs/PLAYBACK.md §9). Unset = tier default.
LOOMBRE_MAX_TRANSCODES=

# Positive integer, bits per second. Caps every session's selected
# bitrate regardless of what the client's DeviceProfile/network conditions
# would otherwise allow. Unset = no server-side cap (device/network only).
LOOMBRE_MAX_STREAM_BITRATE=

# "true" | "false" (default off). Gate-1 capability flag (STATE.md P1.19)
# — while off, restricted-content code paths stay inert and restricted
# libraries cannot be created at all. Read docs/PLAN.md §6.4 before
# enabling on a shared/family instance.
LOOMBRE_RESTRICTED_ENABLED=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Worker — scan/transcode concurrency (docs/PLAN.md §9.2 per-tier caps)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Positive integer. Hash-pool / probe-job concurrency. Unset = auto:
# max(2, detected_cpu_count / 2).
LOOMBRE_SCAN_CONCURRENCY=

# Positive integer. How many 'transcode' jobs THIS worker process runs
# concurrently (a soft cap on top of the server's own admission control —
# see LOOMBRE_MAX_TRANSCODES above, which is the REAL limit). Unset = 8.
LOOMBRE_TRANSCODE_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=

# Integer 1-1000, milliseconds. Poll interval for each active transcode
# session's throttle/seek control loop. Unset = 250. You should not need
# to change this.
LOOMBRE_TRANSCODE_POLL_MS=

# "1" | "0" (only those exact strings are honored —
# apps/worker/src/scan/watcher.ts). "1" forces filesystem POLLING for
# every watched library path: THE escape hatch when your media lives on a
# NAS/SMB/NFS mount bind-mounted into the containers — host-side inotify
# events never reach a container watching a network filesystem, so a
# native watch silently sees nothing and new files are only picked up by
# manual rescans. "0" forces native watching everywhere. Unset/empty =
# the per-path heuristic.
LOOMBRE_SCAN_POLL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ffmpeg/ffprobe overrides
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The image already vendors a pinned, checksum-verified static ffmpeg/
# ffprobe pair for its own architecture at build time (installers/
# ffmpeg-manifest.json, fetched by scripts/fetch-ffmpeg.mjs) and sets
# LOOMBRE_FFMPEG=/usr/local/bin/ffmpeg / LOOMBRE_FFPROBE=/usr/local/bin/ffprobe
# in the Dockerfile — you do not need to set these to get a working
# instance. They exist here as an ESCAPE HATCH: if you need a build with
# hardware-acceleration support the vendored binary lacks (VAAPI on a
# specific GPU, NVENC against host NVIDIA drivers you've bind-mounted in,
# etc.), bind-mount your own ffmpeg/ffprobe into BOTH the `server` and
# `worker` containers (the worker is what actually spawns them; both must
# resolve the same pair) and point these at that container-side path.
#
# NOTE (installer completeness audit): until that audit these two lines
# were documented here but NEVER reached the containers — compose
# `--env-file` values are interpolation-only, not injected — so setting
# them silently did nothing. docker-compose.prod.yml now passes both
# through to server AND worker, with the image's vendored path as the
# default (unset = exact prior behavior). Uncomment and adjust both
# together:
# LOOMBRE_FFMPEG=/usr/local/bin/ffmpeg
# LOOMBRE_FFPROBE=/usr/local/bin/ffprobe

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Worker — metadata providers (docs/PLAN.md §8.3, STATE.md P1.9). Both
# optional — the worker boots and scans fine with neither set; the
# corresponding provider just registers as disabled (logged at boot, and
# surfaced to admins via GET /system/capabilities). MusicBrainz needs no
# key at all.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
LOOMBRE_TMDB_API_KEY=
LOOMBRE_TVDB_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Advanced / rarely needed
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Compose project name — changes the named-volume prefix (loombre_pgdata,
# loombre_data become <name>_pgdata/<name>_data) and container name prefix.
# Only relevant if you're running more than one Loombre stack on the same
# Docker host. Default: "loombre".
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=loombre

# Overrides the image tag docker-compose.prod.yml's `server`/`worker`
# services run (both from the SAME image — see the Dockerfile's header
# comment for why; the `web` service has its OWN image, next line). Only
# needed if you're pulling a specific published tag instead of building
# locally (`docker compose build`).
# LOOMBRE_IMAGE=loombre:latest

# Same, for the `web` service (the web client is a separate image built
# from the same Dockerfile's `web` stage — published releases:
# ghcr.io/loombre/loombre-web).
# LOOMBRE_WEB_IMAGE=loombre-web:latest
